/*! @page internal_table_access Direct access to internal files

Applications should use logical \c table: or \c layered: URIs rather than the internal files
that implement them. Direct access can bypass the logical view or corrupt internal state.

@section internal_table_access_targets Protected files

- Local metadata: \c file:WiredTiger.wt, used in both ordinary and disaggregated databases.
- Local history store: \c file:WiredTigerHS.wt, protected in both database modes.
- Shared metadata: \c file:WiredTigerShared.wt_stable.
- Shared history store: \c file:WiredTigerSharedHS.wt_stable.
- Disaggregated ingest and stable constituent naming patterns: \c *.wt_ingest and \c *.wt_stable.

Metadata and history store object names and their checkpoint views are reserved in every database
mode after URI prefixes and table-name qualifiers have been removed.
Lookalike prefixes are not classified as metadata or history stores. Constituent naming patterns
are checked independently in disaggregated databases regardless of the URI prefix, including
\c table:, \c layered:, \c colgroup: and \c index:.
The public read-only \c metadata: and \c metadata:create cursors remain available.

@section internal_table_access_checks Access checks

The following checks reject access to reserved internal names with \c EINVAL:

<table>
@hrow{API, Ingest/stable constituents, Metadata/history store files, Builds}
@row{WT_SESSION::open_cursor, Rejected, Rejected, Diagnostic only}
@row{WT_SESSION::create\, WT_SESSION::drop\, WT_SESSION::alter\,
    WT_SESSION::truncate (URI form)\, WT_SESSION::compact (URI form), Rejected, Rejected, All}
@row{WT_SESSION::verify\, WT_SESSION::salvage, Rejected, Not rejected by these checks, All}
</table>

Cursor checks include statistics cursors on protected names, checkpoint cursors, checkpoint-view
URIs, duplicated cursors, and cached cursor opens. Truncate checks apply only when a URI is supplied;
ranges specified by existing cursors are not checked again. Internal cursor opens and nested API
calls used to implement logical tables are unaffected.

Schema APIs use the standard object-name validation. Diagnostic cursor checks use the same URI
validation for every prefix, excluding table and index projection lists from the object name.
Create also checks an explicit \c source URI, preventing aliases to protected internal data sources.

Allowing a metadata or history store file through the verify/salvage check does not guarantee that
the operation is supported or succeeds: its usual configuration, storage and handle restrictions
still apply. Non-diagnostic builds omit the cursor check, not the schema or maintenance checks.

@section internal_table_access_defaults Default inspection and maintenance access

The following interfaces deliberately retain internal access without an application session opt-in:

<table>
@hrow{Interface, Default access, Limits}
@row{WT_SESSION::verify / WT_SESSION::salvage and corresponding \c wt commands,
    Metadata and history store files,
    Direct ingest/stable targets still require the bypass. Verify dump options follow the same rule.}
@row{<code>wt dump</code> / <code>wt read</code> / <code>wt stat</code>, All protected data sources,
    The utility session opts in automatically. Read requires string/record-number keys and string values.}
@row{<code>tools/wt_cmp_uri.py</code> / <code>tools/wt_cmp_dir</code>, Read-only comparisons of protected data sources,
    Comparison sessions opt in automatically; connection and data-format restrictions still apply.}
@row{<code>metadata:</code> / <code>metadata:create</code> cursors and <code>wt list -v</code> / <code>wt list -c</code>,
    Read-only local metadata and checkpoint information,
    These interfaces do not expose direct writes or the contents of the shared metadata table.}
@row{<code>wt page</code>, Disaggregated page inspection through a URI or raw table ID,
    Requires a diagnostic build and page-log-backed data. This path does not open a public table cursor.}
@row{<code>wt turtle</code>, Shared turtle page and checkpoint metadata,
    Requires disaggregated storage; uses the connection's page log directly.}
@row{WT_CONNECTION::debug_info with <code>metadata=true</code>\, or handle/cache inspection,
    Local metadata and internal connection state,
    Does not grant application cursors access to internal table records.}
@row{<code>wiredtiger_open</code> with <code>verify_metadata=true</code> or <code>salvage=true</code>\,
    including <code>wt -m</code> / <code>wt -S</code>,
    Metadata verification or salvage during connection open,
    Normal recovery and database-mode restrictions apply.}
@row{<code>wiredtiger_repair</code>: <code>fetch_metadata</code> / <code>fetch_database_size</code>,
    Metadata or database-size inspection through internal paths,
    Shared metadata and database-size commands require a valid disaggregated checkpoint.}
@row{<code>wiredtiger_repair</code>: <code>fix_btree_size</code>,
    Constituent verification and size repair using an opted-in temporary session,
    Requires a valid disaggregated checkpoint. An all-file scan skips shared metadata and shared HS.}
</table>

Logical-table operations, checkpoint, backup, recovery, rollback-to-stable, history store cleanup
and background compaction use internal paths or internal sessions as needed. They are unaffected
by the direct-name checks; this does not authorize direct application access to their internal files.
Verify without a URI visits logical table/layered entries, not every standalone internal file.
Background compaction can consider the history store through its internal session, unlike a direct
application compact call on that file.

Database-mode restrictions remain independent: for example, the \c wt utility does not support the
salvage command or \c -S in disaggregated mode. Log inspection (<code>wt printlog</code> / <code>log:</code>),
timestamp queries and offline file decoders are outside these protected-table cursor checks.

@section internal_table_access_other_tools Tools retaining the normal guards

- Schema-changing commands (<code>wt create</code>, <code>alter</code>, <code>drop</code>,
  <code>truncate</code>, <code>compact</code> and <code>load</code>) retain their production checks.
  Record writes through cursors (<code>wt write</code> / <code>loadtext</code>) follow the diagnostic
  cursor-open rule; they are not automatically opted in.
- <code>tools/wt_verify/wt_verify.py</code> inherits the verify access policy, including its dump modes.
  Its \c -i option names an input file, not the \c wt internal-access flag; it can consume output
  obtained separately with <code>wt -i verify</code>.
- The WiredTiger MCP server (<code>tools/wt-mcp/server.py</code>) uses an ordinary session for cursor
  and statistics tools. Its metadata tools use the public metadata interface; verify-based tools
  inherit the verify exemption. It currently exposes no session-level internal-access configuration.

A read-only connection or cursor does not itself bypass these checks. The named inspection utilities
opt in explicitly; schema-changing commands and the server's ordinary session are not automatically
exempted.

@section internal_table_access_bypass Debugging bypass

Configure an individual session with <code>debug=(allow_internal_access=true)</code>, either when opening it
with WT_CONNECTION::open_session or later with WT_SESSION::reconfigure. This bypasses all checks
above; <code>debug=(allow_internal_access=false)</code> restores them. The \c wt utility provides the same
opt-in through its global \c -i option. Internal WiredTiger sessions are exempt.

The bypass is intended for deliberate debugging, testing and repair, not normal application use.
It does not remove other API restrictions. These checks are guardrails, not a security boundary.

*/
